     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "I've marked the run anomalous. Oxygen in the exhaust has risen steadily through the growth stage, from 3.7 ppm at t=2520 s to 26.0 ppm at t=3240 s. That's about six times the 4–5 ppm level after the purge. Over the same period, argon flow held at 100 sccm and temperature held at 750 °C with steady heater power, so neither explains the rise.\n\n- **Likely cause:** air getting into the tube, such as a leaking seal. That fits a steady O2 rise with normal flow and temperature best. I left the specific cause as undetermined because this furnace has no pressure gauge. That gauge is what would tell a leak apart from a blocked exhaust, and the growth stage needs it to confirm the atmosphere.\n- **Scientific evidence:** there's no characterization yet. Even when there is, a sample grown in this oxygen level may not count as a fair test of the recipe.\n- **Proposed action:** call a human (recorded only; the run continues). They should do a leak check or put a portable pressure gauge on the tube to confirm the cause."
     }
    ],
